# Post-merge branch and folder cleanup

Run this after a PR has been merged (typically the one opened by `/iflow-close`). It detects the merge, switches back to the default branch, and — with a **consolidated confirm** — deletes every local branch that git agrees is merged. Branches that landed via a **squash merge** need a **second, dedicated confirm**, because their tips are not reachable from the default branch and `git branch -d` refuses them.

With trailing text like `include GitHub`{% if cleanup_include_github %}, or when this project bakes `cleanup_include_github = true`,{% endif %} it also runs a **GitHub remote-branch audit** (Phase B): classify `origin/*`, summarise unique work, and optionally delete deletable remotes and/or file a findings issue under a **second** confirm. Opt out of a baked-on Phase B with `no github` / `local only`.

`/iflow-cleanup` is the only command in the workflow that touches branches destructively: locally via `git branch -d` (Phase A1) and `git branch -D` for provably-landed squash merges (Phase A2, its own confirm, tip SHAs always printed); remotely via `git push origin --delete` only after the Phase B confirm, never `--force`.

