## Input

Optional free-form text after the command. Examples:

- **No extra text** — detect the current branch's PR, clean that up, plus any other local branches already merged into the default (Phase A{% if cleanup_include_github %}; Phase B also runs by default because `cleanup_include_github = true`{% else %} only{% endif %}).
- A branch name — clean up that specific branch instead of the current one (e.g. `/iflow-cleanup 42-fix-login`).
- **GitHub audit opt-in tokens** (case-insensitive): `include github`, `include gh`, `with github`, or standalone `github` — also run Phase B after Phase A.
- **GitHub audit opt-out tokens** (case-insensitive): `no github`, `local only`, `local-only` — skip Phase B even when `cleanup_include_github` is baked true.

**Phase B enable rule:** (`cleanup_include_github` baked true **or** opt-in token) **and** no opt-out token.

## Steps

1. **Detect the default branch.**
   - Prefer `gh repo view --json defaultBranchRef -q .defaultBranchRef.name`.
   - Else `git symbolic-ref --quiet --short refs/remotes/origin/HEAD | sed 's|^origin/||'`.
   - Else fall back to `main`.

2. **Identify the branch to clean.**
   - If the user passed a branch name (ignoring GitHub-audit tokens), use it.
   - Else use the current branch (`git branch --show-current`). If the current branch **is** the default, skip to step 7 (group sweep only); there is no issue branch to merge-check.

3. **Check PR / merge state for the issue branch.**
   - Prefer `gh pr view <branch> --json state,mergedAt,mergeCommit,headRefName`.
   - If `gh` is unavailable, approximate with `git fetch --prune` then `git cherry origin/<default> <branch>`. All commits prefixed `-` means the branch is effectively merged (including squash-merges).
   - **If the branch is not merged:**
     - Remind the user that the working copy is still on the issue branch (not the default). Suggest `git switch <default>` before starting unrelated work.
     - Tell them to re-run `/iflow-cleanup` after the PR merges.
     - **Stop Phase A.** Do not delete anything locally. If Phase B is enabled (see Input), Phase B may still run (remote audit does not require the issue branch to be merged).

4. **Classify the local branches.**
   - Prefer `issue-flow agent local-branches --json` — read-only, buckets each local branch as `reachable` / `squash_landed` / `merged_pr_divergent` / `unique_work` / `skipped`, with a `tip` short SHA on every entry.
   - Manual fallback per branch (skip the current and default branches): `git merge-base --is-ancestor <branch> origin/<default>` (exit 0 → `reachable`); else `git cherry origin/<default> <branch>` with no `+` lines → `squash_landed`; else check `gh pr list --state all --head <branch> --json number,state,mergedAt,url` and compare `git log --no-merges --format=%cI origin/<default>..<branch>` to `mergedAt` — a merged PR with nothing newer → `merged_pr_divergent`, otherwise `unique_work`.
   - `git branch -d` applies git's **reachability** test, while `git cherry` proves **patch equivalence**. A {% if pr_merge_method == "squash" %}squash merge (this project's `pr_merge_method`){% else %}squash merge{% endif %} rewrites commits onto a new commit, so the branch tip stays unreachable and `-d` refuses it no matter how thoroughly the work landed.

5. **Consolidated confirm for post-merge cleanup (Phase A1).** Gather the full action list into a single yes/no prompt:
   - `git switch <default>`
   - `git pull --ff-only`
   - `git fetch --prune`
   - `git branch -d <branch>` for each **`reachable`** branch, listed explicitly by name before asking.
   - **Planned release tag (tag-derived projects only).** When `/iflow-close` planned a tag it did not create — check the focus issue's status file and the newest `{{ history_file }}` release section for a version whose tag is missing from `git tag -l` — include creating it in the same confirm: `git tag <planned>` then `git push origin <planned>` (or `gh release create <planned> --generate-notes`). Run it **after** the pull so the tag lands on the merged {{ pr_merge_method }} commit.
   - If `-d` still refuses a branch, report it and leave it alone — do not escalate to `-D` here.

6. **Dedicated force-delete confirm (Phase A2).** Only when `squash_landed` or `merged_pr_divergent` is non-empty; skip the step silently otherwise.
   - Explain that these branches need `git branch -D` because the {{ pr_merge_method }} merge left no reachable tip, and that `-D` bypasses git's safety check.
   - List `squash_landed` as `<name>  <tip>`, and list `merged_pr_divergent` **separately** with each branch's merged PR link and unique-commit subjects so the user can see what differs.
   - Include the recovery line: `git branch <name> <tip>` restores anything deleted.
   - Ask **once**, separately from Phase A1. On yes, try `git branch -d <name>` first and fall back to `-D` only when it refuses (`-d` also accepts branches merged into their own upstream, so it can still succeed while the remote-tracking ref exists); report every `<name> <tip>` and the flag used. On no, nothing is deleted.
   - Never list a `unique_work` or `skipped` branch here, whatever the user asks for.

7. **Optional folder sweep** (safe; no destructive git).
   - Group files in `{{ issueflows_dir }}/{{ current_issues_folder }}/` by issue number (`issue<N>_*`).
   - For each group whose status file contains `- [x] Done` (case-insensitive on `done`), move the group to `{{ issueflows_dir }}/{{ solved_folder }}/`.
   - Groups without a checked `Done` stay put — do not route them to `{{ issueflows_dir }}/{{ partly_solved_folder }}/` from `/iflow-cleanup`. That is `/iflow-pause`'s job and requires a human decision.

8. **Epic stage gate (offer only).**
   - If the just-merged issue's number appears in a `- Published: #<N>` line of an `epic<M>_plan.md` under `{{ issueflows_dir }}/{{ epics_folder }}/`, it belongs to an epic. Run `issue-flow agent epic-status <M> --json`; if the issue's stage now has no open issues left, the stage just completed.
   - On a completed stage, **offer** (never automatic): (a) post a short stage-summary comment on the epic anchor issue, and (b) run `/iflow-epic <M> publish` to publish the next stage. Publishing and commenting stay the user's explicit call.

9. **Phase B — GitHub remote audit** (only when Phase B is enabled per the Input enable rule).
   - Prefer `issue-flow agent branches --json` (read-only classification of `origin/*` into `deletable` / `unique_work` / `skipped`). Manual fallback: `git fetch --prune`, `git cherry` / `git log --oneline` / `git diff --shortstat`, plus `gh pr list --state all --head <branch>`.
   - Report buckets; summarise unique-work commit subjects and open PR titles.
   - **Its own consolidated confirm** (never implied by the Phase A1 or A2 yes):
     - Optional `git push origin --delete <branch>` for each listed **deletable** remote — never `--force`, never the default branch.
     - Optional findings issue via `gh issue create` after showing draft title/body (suggested title: `chore: remote branch audit (<YYYY-MM-DD>)`).
   - Declining leaves remotes and GitHub issues untouched.

## Output

Report:
- default branch resolved
- PR/merge status of the target branch
- post-merge commands run (if any)
- branch classification counts (`reachable` / `squash_landed` / `merged_pr_divergent` / `unique_work` / `skipped`)
- list of local branches deleted with `-d` (Phase A1)
- list of local branches deleted with `-D` and their tip SHAs (Phase A2), or "declined" / "none offered"
- list of branches left alone, with a one-line reason (unique work, `-d` refused, unclassifiable)
- folder sweep summary (`issue<N>` → `{{ solved_folder }}/`, or "nothing to sweep")
- when Phase B ran: deletable / unique-work / skipped counts, remote deletes, findings issue URL or "skipped"

## Constraints

- Never use `git push --force`.
- `git branch -D` only for `squash_landed` / `merged_pr_divergent` branches, only after the Phase A2 confirm, and only with tip SHAs reported. Never `-D` unique work, an unclassifiable branch, or the current branch. A `-d` refusal in Phase A1 is reported, never force-retried.
- Never delete the default branch (local or remote).
- Remote deletes and findings-issue creation require the Phase B confirm. None of the three confirms implies another.
- If anything is ambiguous (detached HEAD, multiple remotes, missing tracking info), report and stop rather than guessing.
- Do not open or update PRs. Do not bump version fields — pyproject bumps belong to `/iflow-close`. The only version action allowed here is creating a release tag that `/iflow-close` **planned** (tag-derived strategy), inside the Phase A consolidated confirm.
- Do **not** offer to update `{{ history_file }}` / CHANGELOG here — that belongs in `/iflow-close` before the PR.
